# Quellwerk

> **20-second pitch:** Quellwerk is a local-first research workspace for PDFs, websites, YouTube transcripts, text and notes. It combines BM25 with multilingual embeddings and optional cross-encoder reranking, streams source-grounded answers, and lets users jump from every citation to the exact page, timestamp or text passage.

[![CI](https://github.com/dominik13579/quellwerk/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/dominik13579/quellwerk/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-01696f.svg)](LICENSE)

<!-- Replace these before publishing. -->
**[Live demo](https://YOUR-DEMO-URL.example)** · **[60-second video](https://YOUR-VIDEO-URL.example)**

![Quellwerk source-grounded research flow](docs/quellwerk-demo.gif)

## Why it exists

General chat interfaces make it difficult to inspect why an answer was produced. Quellwerk keeps original sources, retrieval results and citations connected: a citation opens the corresponding PDF page, YouTube timestamp or highlighted passage.

## Highlights

- Multi-source ingestion: PDF, website, YouTube, pasted text and notes.
- Hybrid retrieval: BM25 + multilingual E5 embeddings + Reciprocal Rank Fusion + optional cross-encoder reranking.
- Streaming answers over Server-Sent Events.
- Inspectable citations with source-type-specific locators.
- Local-first browser persistence through IndexedDB.
- Hardened website imports with URL/IP validation, per-hop redirect validation, HTML-only imports and byte limits.
- Server-side model allowlist, output-token cap, per-minute rate limit and daily demo limit.
- Unit, integration and Playwright browser tests plus a bilingual RAG evaluation harness.

## Demo

The recommended public demo is deliberately restricted:

- It starts with the notebook in `demo/notebook.json`.
- User uploads/imports are disabled with `ALLOW_USER_SOURCES=0`.
- Only models in `ALLOWED_MODELS` are accepted by the backend.
- `MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS`, `RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE` and `DAILY_CHAT_LIMIT` bound usage.
- The API key exists only as a host secret.

This is a portfolio demo, not a multi-tenant production service. Do not upload confidential documents.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    U[Browser UI] -->|sources + query| API[FastAPI]
    U --> DB[(IndexedDB)]
    API --> I[Import security]
    I --> W[Web / YouTube]
    API --> R[Hybrid retrieval]
    R --> B[BM25]
    R --> E[Multilingual embeddings]
    R --> X[Cross-encoder reranker]
    API --> P[OpenRouter adapter]
    P -->|SSE tokens + citations| U
```

### Query path

1. The browser turns every source into chunks with typed locators.
2. The backend retrieves candidates with BM25 and optional dense embeddings.
3. Reciprocal Rank Fusion combines rankings; the cross-encoder can rerank candidates.
4. Only top passages enter the model context.
5. Tokens and retrieval metadata stream to the browser; citation clicks reopen the source locator.

## Quickstart

### Docker

```bash
cp .env.example .env
# Add OPENROUTER_API_KEY to .env
docker compose up --build
```

Open <http://localhost:8000>. Stop with `docker compose down`.

### Local Python

Python 3.12 is recommended.

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate          # Windows PowerShell: .venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e '.[test]'
cp .env.example .env
uvicorn app:app --reload
```

The first full RAG query can download embedding and reranker models. For a lightweight deterministic BM25-only run, set `RAG_DISABLE_MODELS=1`.

## Tests

Install browser binaries once:

```bash
python -m playwright install chromium
```

Run the fast suite during development:

```bash
pytest -m 'not e2e and not live'
```

Run one area or one exact test:

```bash
pytest tests/test_security.py -v
pytest tests/test_retrieval.py::test_retrieve_bm25_returns_relevant_chunk_first -v
```

Run coverage:

```bash
pytest -m 'not e2e and not live' --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-report=html
# Open htmlcov/index.html
```

Run the browser flow headlessly or visibly:

```bash
pytest -m e2e --browser chromium
pytest -m e2e --browser chromium --headed --slowmo 300
```

The Playwright test starts its own FastAPI server and mocks `/api/chat`, so it needs no provider key and incurs no cost. It executes **text import → question → streamed answer → citation click → passage highlight**. CI runs the same suites on every push and pull request and uploads traces/screenshots when the browser test fails.

## RAG evaluation

The repository includes a small manually checked, fictional corpus with German and English answerable/unanswerable questions. Fictional facts make the gold labels unambiguous and avoid redistributing private or copyrighted documents.

Deterministic retrieval baseline:

```bash
RAG_DISABLE_MODELS=1 python evaluation/run.py
cat evaluation/results/metrics.json
```

Full answer evaluation against a running instance:

```bash
python evaluation/run.py --live-url http://localhost:8000 --model openrouter/free
# Review evaluation/results/human_review.csv
python evaluation/summarize_review.py
```

The run documents Recall@1/3/5, MRR, retrieval and answer latency, citation precision, groundedness and error rate. Automated groundedness is labeled as a proxy; portfolio claims should use the completed human review. See [`evaluation/README.md`](evaluation/README.md) for the scoring protocol.

### Results

The tiny BM25 smoke run achieved Recall@1/3/5 and MRR of 1.00 with no retrieval errors on this deliberately simple 12-question fixture. Treat this as regression evidence, not a general quality claim; latency is omitted because a local micro-benchmark is hardware-dependent. Run and review the hybrid answer evaluation before publishing stronger portfolio claims.

| Run | Retrieval mode | Recall@5 | MRR | Citation precision | Groundedness | Mean answer latency | Error rate |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 2026-10-01 local smoke run | BM25 baseline | 1.00 | 1.00 | — | — | — | 0.00 |
| Pending human review | Hybrid + reranker | — | — | — | — | — | — |

## Security model

### Protected

- OpenRouter credentials stay in environment variables on the server.
- Website import accepts only HTTP(S) ports 80/443 with no URL credentials.
- DNS results must be globally routable; every redirect destination is revalidated.
- Website responses must be HTML and remain below `MAX_IMPORT_BYTES`.
- Chat requests have context, model, output-token and rate limits.
- Rendered model/source text is HTML-escaped before insertion.

### Trust boundaries

- Imported documents are untrusted content and may contain prompt-injection attempts.
- OpenRouter receives the retrieved source passages needed to answer a question.
- IndexedDB is local to the browser profile; it is not encrypted by Quellwerk.
- The in-memory rate limiter is suitable for a single demo process, not distributed production.

For a real multi-user release, add authentication, durable per-user quotas, a reverse proxy/WAF, isolated ingestion workers, malware scanning, encrypted server persistence, audit logs and provider-level budget alerts.

## Trade-offs

| Decision | Benefit | Cost / next step |
|---|---|---|
| Browser-side PDF parsing | Files remain local for viewing | Large PDFs consume client memory; add a worker and strict byte limit. |
| IndexedDB | No account or database required | No cross-device sync or collaboration. |
| Client-provided chunks | Simple local-first MVP | Payloads are large and ingestion repeats; move indexing server-side for scale. |
| OpenRouter | Fast access to multiple models | External data processor, cost and availability dependency. |
| Optional model fallback | App can run BM25-only | Retrieval mode must be visible in production telemetry. |
| Vanilla JS | Small stack and transparent behavior | `app.js` should be split into modules as the product grows. |

## Known limitations

- PDF extraction does not yet include OCR, tables or layout-aware reading order.
- Website extraction is heuristic and some sites disallow crawling or embedding.
- The benchmark is intentionally small and should not be presented as general model research.
- Daily quotas reset in process memory and do not coordinate across replicas.
- There is no authentication or collaboration layer.
- Prompt-injection defenses need a larger adversarial evaluation before handling sensitive workflows.

## Roadmap

- [ ] Claim Ledger: claim-by-claim supporting, conflicting or missing evidence.
- [ ] Citation Inspector with scores, selected passage and user feedback.
- [ ] Persisted document index and embedding cache.
- [ ] OCR and layout-aware PDF parsing.
- [ ] Authentication, durable quotas and per-user notebooks.
- [ ] OpenTelemetry metrics/traces and provider cost dashboard.
- [ ] Accessibility audit and keyboard-complete dialogs/source rows.

## Repository hygiene

Before the first push:

```bash
./scripts/check-secrets.sh
git status --ignored
```

If an API key was ever committed, removing the file in a later commit is not enough: rotate the key and purge it from Git history. Store deployment keys in the hosting provider's secret manager; GitHub Actions secrets are only needed when a workflow itself deploys or calls a protected service.

